<h2>The trade-off</h2>
<p>The upside is simple. You pay no LMI, and you can buy years sooner than if you waited to save 20%. The government describes the LMI you avoid as "tens of thousands of dollars".</p>
<p>The cost is a bigger loan.</p>
{% call v.stats() %}
<li><span class="meta av-label">Illustrative</span><strong><span class="av-fig">about $630</span> a month more</strong></li>
<li><span class="meta av-label">Illustrative</span><strong><span class="av-fig">$120,000+</span> more interest</strong></li>
<li><span class="meta av-label">Illustrative</span><strong><span class="av-fig">No LMI</span></strong></li>
{% endcall %}
<p><strong>Illustrative example only.</strong> A $700,000 purchase. An assumed rate of 6.0%, 30 years, principal and interest. This is not a lender rate or a quote.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th></th><th>5% deposit</th><th>20% deposit</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Loan</td><td>$665,000</td><td>$560,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Monthly repayment</td><td>about $3,987</td><td>about $3,357</td></tr>
<tr><td>Total interest over 30 years</td><td>about $770,000</td><td>about $649,000</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>That is roughly $630 a month more, and more than $120,000 extra in interest over the life of the loan.</p>
<p>A bigger loan also means:</p>
<ul>
<li>Tighter servicing. The lender has to be satisfied you can repay a loan of up to 95% of the value.</li>
<li>Rate rises hit harder.</li>
<li>Less equity buffer if values fall.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Lenders</h2>
<p>Only Participating Lenders can offer the scheme. There are more than 30. The <a href="https://www.firsthomebuyers.gov.au/australian-government-5-percent-deposit-scheme/5-percent-participating-lenders">official list of participating lenders</a> is the one to trust.</p>
{% call v.note("ink") %}
<p>Being eligible for the scheme is not loan approval. Each lender still applies its own rules on servicing, deposit, valuation, pricing and products.</p>
{% endcall %}
<p>A few scheme rules apply across the board:</p>
<ul>
<li>No interest-only loans, except in limited cases such as construction.</li>
<li>No top-ups after settlement.</li>
</ul>
<p>Features like offset accounts and fixed rates vary by lender.</p>
<h2>Common traps</h2>
{% call v.checklist() %}
<li><strong>The cap covers the valuation too.</strong> If the price is under the cap but the valuation comes in over it, you have a problem. For land plus a separate build contract, the combined cost counts.</li>
<li><strong>Occupancy.</strong> Not moving in within 6 months, or moving out or renting it out without an exemption, can end the guarantee. The lender may then charge LMI.</li>
<li><strong>The 10-year look-back is wide.</strong> It covers freehold, leases of 50 years or more, and company title, anywhere in Australia and of any type, including commercial and investment.</li>
<li><strong>The guarantee ends.</strong> It stops when your scheduled loan balance reaches 80% of the original property value. Extra repayments you can redraw do not count. It also ends if you top up, sell, or refinance to a non-participating lender. Moving to another participating lender can keep it, if the loan is not increased and the term is not extended.</li>
<li><strong>You must put in your savings.</strong> You are expected to contribute as much as you can. If you have 20% or more after costs, you are not eligible. Moving money to someone else to get under 20% breaches the intent of the scheme.</li>
<li><strong>Costs sit on top.</strong> Stamp duty, legal fees and bank fees are on top of the 5%.</li>
<li><strong>State help.</strong> State grants and stamp duty concessions can generally be used alongside the scheme. The rules differ by state.</li>
{% endcall %}
<h2>How it differs from Help to Buy and a private 2.5% facility</h2>
<p>With the 5% Deposit Scheme, the government guarantees part of your loan to the lender. It takes no stake in your home. You keep all the growth.</p>
<p><strong>Help to Buy</strong> is federal shared equity. The government owns a share of your home. It opened on 5 December 2025. More in my <a href="/articles/help-to-buy-what-it-is/">Help to Buy explainer</a>.</p>
<p><strong>HAS SmartShare</strong> is a private product, not a government scheme. I covered it in <a href="/articles/buy-with-a-tiny-deposit/">buying with a tiny deposit</a>.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th></th><th>5% Deposit Scheme</th><th>Help to Buy</th><th>HAS SmartShare (private)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Who runs it</td><td>Australian Government (Housing Australia)</td><td>Australian Government</td><td>HAS, a private provider</td></tr>
<tr><td>Minimum deposit</td><td>5% (2% single parents)</td><td>2%</td><td>2.5% plus costs</td></tr>
<tr><td>How it works</td><td>Guarantee to your lender, up to 15%</td><td>Government owns up to 30% (existing) or 40% (new)</td><td>17.5% second mortgage, main lender at 80%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Who owns the home</td><td>You</td><td>You and the government</td><td>You. HAS is not on title</td></tr>
<tr><td>Growth</td><td>All yours</td><td>Shared with the government</td><td>HAS takes an agreed share of growth above 10%</td></tr>
<tr><td>Losses</td><td>All yours</td><td>Shared with the government</td><td>All yours. HAS does not share losses</td></tr>
<tr><td>Income test</td><td>None</td><td>$103,000 single, $165,000 joint or single parent (2026-27)</td><td>Not a scheme test</td></tr>
<tr><td>Price range</td><td>Caps by location</td><td>NSW $1.3m / $800k, NT $600k, other states match the 5% scheme</td><td>Properties $600k to $1.6m</td></tr>
<tr><td>Places</td><td>Unlimited</td><td>10,000 a year</td><td>Not a scheme limit</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Help to Buy is for citizens only, and you cannot own property in Australia or overseas. Only a few lenders offer it: Bank Australia, CommBank, the Teachers Mutual group and Queensland Country Bank. You cannot combine it with the 5% Deposit Scheme.</p>
<p>HAS SmartShare is fixed rate and interest-only on its portion for the first 5 years. It has a 3-year minimum term with an early-exit fee, and independent legal advice is required.</p>
<h2>When it suits a Sydney professional, and when to leave it</h2>
{% call v.compare("av-compare--pair") %}
{% call v.card("", "oak") %}
<p><strong>It tends to suit you when:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Your income is strong but your savings are not. Servicing a 95% loan is realistic, saving 20% is years away.</li>
<li>The place you want is under the $1.5 million Sydney cap, and the valuation is likely to land there too.</li>
<li>You plan to live in the home for years, not rent it out soon.</li>
</ul>
{% endcall %}
{% call v.card("", "clay") %}
<p><strong>It may be worth leaving when:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>You are already close to 20%. If you have 20% or more after costs, you are not eligible anyway. If you are just under, the extra interest on a bigger loan may outweigh waiting a few months.</li>
<li>The property is over the cap, or close enough that a valuation could tip it over.</li>
<li>A move or posting elsewhere is likely soon, which would put the occupancy rule at risk.</li>
<li>The repayment on 95% only works if rates never rise.</li>
</ul>
{% endcall %}
{% endcall %}
